The textbook mainly describes electron-vacuum devices that use a heated cathode as an electron source.
Heating a suitable material gives some electrons enough energy to leave its surface;

this is the thermionic emission mentioned in § 103 of the book.
The electrons enter the vacuum region from the cathode and move under a suitable electric field;

a continuous current also requires the external circuit to replenish charge.
A heated cathode is a common electron source, not the only way to supply electrons to a vacuum.
